<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ernest Renan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1823">1823</a>–<a href="/years/1892">1892</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ernest Renan was a French scholar of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/semitic-languages">Semitic languages</a>, historian of religion and essayist. In 1860–1861 he led a French archaeological mission to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lebanon">Lebanon</a> that explored <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byblos">Byblos</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyre">Tyre</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sidon">Sidon</a>, published as Mission de Phénicie. His Life of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a> of 1863 caused a sensation by treating Jesus as a historical man, and his lecture What Is a Nation? of 1882 remains a classic text on nationhood.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ernest-renan.jpg" alt="Ernest Renan" width="320" height="400" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Antoine Samuel Adam-Salomon · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ernest_Renan_1876-84.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Joseph Ernest Renan was born on 27 February 1823 at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treguier">Tréguier</a> in Brittany, into a family of fishermen. His father, the captain of a small cutter and an ardent republican, died when he was five, and his sister Henriette, twelve years older, became the moral head of the household. He was educated at the ecclesiastical seminary of Tréguier, and in 1838 Félix Dupanloup brought him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>. In 1840 he moved to the Saint-Sulpice Seminary to study philosophy. Studying <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syriac">Syriac</a> from 1844, he concluded that the second part of the Book of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isaiah">Isaiah</a> differs from the first in date and that the Book of Daniel was written centuries after its setting. In October 1845 he left Saint-Sulpice and became a teacher.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1847 Renan won the Volney prize for his General History of Semitic Languages and took his degree as agrégé de philosophie. In 1856 he married Cornélie Scheffer, and they had two children, Ary and Noémi. In 1869 he stood for the liberal opposition at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meaux">Meaux</a> but was not elected. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franco-prussian-war">Franco-Prussian War</a> was a turning point: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, which he had seen as the home of disinterested science, now appeared to him as an invader. In the 1870s he rallied to the French Third Republic. In 1883 he published the autobiographical Souvenirs d'enfance et de jeunesse, which with the Life of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a> is the work by which he is chiefly known.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>His Histoire des origines du christianisme, published between 1863 and 1881, ran from the Life of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a> through The Apostles and Saint Paul to Marc-Aurèle, and he later wrote a five-volume Histoire du peuple d'Israël. He held that races differ by instinct and that the Semitic race was inferior to the Indo-European, calling Semites an incomplete race. He nevertheless disputed in 1883 that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> form a unified biological race, and criticised German ethnic nationalism. He supported colonial expansion.</p><p>Anatole <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> called him the incarnation of modernity, and Nietzsche and Jacob Burckhardt attacked his interpretations. A monument to him unveiled in 1903 in the cathedral square of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treguier">Tréguier</a> was read as a challenge to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a> and caused widespread protests.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ernest Renan” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Modern excavation, begun by <b>Ernest Renan</b> in 1860 and continued by Pierre Montet and Maurice Dunand, uncovered layers from the Neolithic to the Crusades, and the site was made a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 1984.</blockquote><a href="/empire/byblos">Byblos · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Ernest Renan</b>'s Mission de Phénicie of 1860 and the Ottoman excavations from 1887 made its necropoleis central to the recovery of Phoenician antiquity, and the royal sarcophagi in Istanbul and the finds in the National Museum in Beirut remain its best-known legacy.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sidon">Sidon · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1882 <b>Ernest Renan</b> wrote that had Christianity been arrested, “the world would have been Mithraic”; Leonard Boyle and J. A. Ezquerra think the threat overstated.</blockquote><a href="/religion/mithraism">Mithraism · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>Modern study began with the decipherment of the script by Jean-Jacques Barthélemy in 1758, from a bilingual dedication to Melqart found on Malta, and with <b>Ernest Renan</b>’s Mission de Phénicie of 1860–1861.</blockquote><a href="/culture/phoenicians">Phoenicians · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>Civic nationalism defines the nation as an association of people with equal and shared political rights, not a common ancestry; <b>Ernest Renan</b>, in his lecture “What is a Nation?” of 1882, called it a “daily referendum” on the will to live together.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/nationalism">Nationalism · Core Ideas</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treguier">Tréguier</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in 5th arrondissement of Paris, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=48.79,-3.23&amp;year=1867" aria-label="Tréguier on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4475 460 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Ernest Renan"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4925 730h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4925 730h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=48.79,-3.23&amp;year=1867">What was here in 1867? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;The one object in life is the development of the mind, and the first condition for the development of the mind is that it should have liberty.&#8221;</p><footer>Recollections of My Youth (1883), preface &#183; translator not named &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/12748/pg12748-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;I no longer believe Christianity to be the supernatural summary of all that man can know; but I still believe that life is the most frivolous of things, unless it is regarded as one great and constant duty.&#8221;</p><footer>Recollections of My Youth (1883) &#183; translator not named &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/12748/pg12748-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;I am double, as it were, and one half of me laughs while the other weeps. This is the explanation of my cheerfulness. As I am two spirits in one body, one of them has always cause to be content.&#8221;</p><footer>Recollections of My Youth (1883) &#183; translator not named &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/12748/pg12748-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><details class="enc-q-more"><summary>1 more</summary><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Its songs of joy end as elegies; there is nothing to equal the delicious sadness of its national melodies.&#8221;</p><footer>The Poetry of the Celtic Races (1854) &#183; tr. W. G. Hutchison, 1896 &#183; of the Celtic peoples, Breton and Welsh among them &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/5637/pg5637-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote></details><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 540 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
